---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { schemaOrganization, SITE_URL as SITE } from '../data/schema';
import { PHONE, WHATSAPP_NUMBER, EMAIL, WHATSAPP_URL } from '../data/schema';

export const prerender = true;

const pageTitle = "Bangladesh vs Vietnam Manufacturing | 2026 Guide | SDF";
const pageDescription = "Bangladesh vs Vietnam clothing manufacturing: costs, MOQ, tariffs, lead times and certifications. Find out which country is right for your brand in 2026.";

const whatsappQuoteUrl = `${WHATSAPP_URL}?text=Hi%20SDF%20Clothing%2C%20I%27d%20like%20a%20quote%20for%20clothing%20manufacturing%20comparison`;

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "WebPage",
      "@id": Astro.url.href,
      "url": Astro.url.href,
      "name": pageTitle,
      "description": pageDescription,
      "datePublished": "2025-01-15",
      "dateModified": "2026-05-21",
      "inLanguage": "en-US",
      "isPartOf": {
        "@type": "WebSite",
        "@id": SITE + "/#website",
        "url": SITE,
        "name": "SDF Clothing"
      },
      "breadcrumb": {
        "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
        "itemListElement": [
          {
            "@type": "ListItem",
            "position": 1,
            "name": "Home",
            "item": SITE
          },
          {
            "@type": "ListItem",
            "position": 2,
            "name": pageTitle,
            "item": Astro.url.href
          }
        ]
      },
      "publisher": { "@id": SITE + "/#org" }
    },
    schemaOrganization,
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      "mainEntity": [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is Bangladesh or Vietnam cheaper for clothing manufacturing?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Bangladesh is generally cheaper for clothing manufacturing due to lower labor costs. Bangladesh's minimum wage for garment workers is approximately $113 per month, while Vietnam ranges from $150-192 monthly depending on region. This labor advantage translates to 10-15% lower FOB prices for basic garments from Bangladesh. However, Vietnam can be competitive for complex woven garments where higher efficiency offsets labor costs."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Which country has lower MOQ | Bangladesh or Vietnam?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Bangladesh has lower minimum order quantities. Typical Bangladesh MOQs range from 300-500 pieces per style, with SDF Clothing offering 300 piece minimums. Vietnam factories typically require 500-1,500 pieces per style. Vietnam's higher MOQs reflect their focus on larger orders and higher production efficiency. For startup brands and small businesses, Bangladesh's lower MOQs provide significant flexibility."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does Bangladesh have duty-free access to the EU?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, Bangladesh currently enjoys duty-free access to the EU under the Everything But Arms (EBA) arrangement for least developed countries. This provides 0% duty on most apparel products. Bangladesh graduates from LDC status in November 2026, which will change this arrangement. Vietnam has the EU-Vietnam Free Trade Agreement (EVFTA) offering 0% duty for qualifying products meeting rules of origin requirements."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What clothing is Bangladesh best at manufacturing?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Bangladesh specializes in knitwear, which represents approximately 70% of the country's apparel exports. Key strengths include t-shirts, hoodies, polo shirts, activewear basics, denim, fleece, and basic woven garments. Bangladesh has developed deep expertise in knitwear production with vertically integrated operations covering knitting, dyeing, and garment production. The country is also strong in basic woven shirts and pants."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How do lead times compare between Bangladesh and Vietnam?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Vietnam generally has shorter lead times due to better port infrastructure and customs efficiency. Vietnam can release raw material imports within 24 hours and deliver to European buyers 10-15 days faster than Bangladesh. Bangladesh takes 48 hours to one month to release raw material imports due to port congestion. Production lead times are similar (30-45 days), but Vietnam's logistics advantage gives them an overall edge in total delivery time."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is quality better in Bangladesh or Vietnam?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Both countries produce high-quality garments, but they excel in different areas. Vietnam has higher worker efficiency (65% vs 40% in Bangladesh) and often performs better on quality consistency metrics for complex woven garments. Bangladesh has made significant quality improvements post-Rana Plaza and excels at knitwear quality. Bangladesh leads in sustainability certifications with the most LEED-certified green factories globally. Quality ultimately depends on the specific factory, not the country."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can small brands work with Bangladesh factories?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, small brands can work with Bangladesh factories, and Bangladesh is often more accommodating than Vietnam for small orders. Many Bangladesh manufacturers, including SDF Clothing, specialize in low MOQ production with 300 piece minimums. Bangladesh factories offer greater flexibility in order quantity and size breakdowns. Vietnam factories typically focus on larger orders due to their higher efficiency model and higher MOQ requirements."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What happens to Bangladesh trade preferences after 2026?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Bangladesh graduates from Least Developed Country status in November 2026, which will remove EU duty-free access under EBA. However, Bangladesh is negotiating new trade agreements with the EU and other markets. The government is preparing for this transition by improving productivity, negotiating bilateral agreements, and encouraging factories to obtain certifications that will be valuable in a post-LDC environment. Many manufacturers are already adapting to the changing trade landscape."
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "Service",
      "serviceType": "Clothing Manufacturing Comparison",
      "provider": { "@id": SITE + "/#org" },
      "areaServed": ["US", "GB", "AU", "CA", "DE", "FR", "IT", "ES"],
      "description": pageDescription
    },
    {
      "@type": "HowTo",
      "name": "How to Choose Between Bangladesh and Vietnam for Clothing Manufacturing",
      "step": [
        {
          "@type": "HowToStep",
          "name": "Evaluate Your MOQ Requirements",
          "text": "Determine your order quantity per style. If you need 300-500 pieces, Bangladesh is more suitable. For orders of 1,000+ pieces, both countries work well. Bangladesh offers greater flexibility for size and color breakdowns within smaller orders."
        },
        {
          "@type": "HowToStep",
          "name": "Consider Your Target Markets",
          "text": "If selling primarily to the EU before November 2026, Bangladesh's EBA duty-free access provides significant cost advantages. For US markets, both countries face similar MFN tariffs. Vietnam's EVFTA provides EU access post-2026, making them attractive for EU-focused brands."
        },
        {
          "@type": "HowToStep",
          "name": "Match Product Type to Country Strengths",
          "text": "Choose Bangladesh for knitwear, t-shirts, activewear basics, and denim. Select Vietnam for complex woven garments, technical outerwear, footwear, and products requiring higher precision. Both countries can produce most garment types, but each has specialization advantages."
        },
        {
          "@type": "HowToStep",
          "name": "Assess Timeline Requirements",
          "text": "If speed is critical and you have tight deadlines, Vietnam's better logistics may provide 10-15 day advantages. If you have longer lead times, Bangladesh's cost advantages may outweigh the delivery time difference. Factor in production time, shipping, and customs clearance."
        },
        {
          "@type": "HowToStep",
          "name": "Review Certification Needs",
          "text": "For sustainability-focused brands requiring GOTS, OEKO-TEX, and green factory certifications, Bangladesh has more certified facilities. Vietnam is improving but has fewer certified options. Verify specific certifications needed for your retail partners and market requirements."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      "name": "Key Comparison Factors Between Bangladesh and Vietnam",
      "itemListElement": [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          "position": 1,
          "name": "Labor Costs"
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          "position": 2,
          "name": "Minimum Order Quantities"
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          "position": 3,
          "name": "Trade Agreements and Tariffs"
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          "position": 4,
          "name": "Product Specialization"
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          "position": 5,
          "name": "Lead Times and Logistics"
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          "position": 6,
          "name": "Sustainability Certifications"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
};
---

<BaseLayout title={pageTitle} description={pageDescription} schema={schema}>

<a href="#main-content" class="skip-link">Skip to main content</a>

<nav aria-label="Breadcrumb">
  <ol class="breadcrumb" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/BreadcrumbList">
    <li class="breadcrumb__item" itemprop="itemListElement" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/ListItem">
      <a href="/" itemprop="item"><span itemprop="name">Home</span></a>
      <meta itemprop="position" content="1" />
    </li>
    <li class="breadcrumb__item breadcrumb__item--current" itemprop="itemListElement" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/ListItem">
      <span itemprop="name">Bangladesh vs Vietnam Manufacturing</span>
      <meta itemprop="position" content="2" />
    </li>
  </ol>
</nav>

<main id="main-content">
  <!-- Hero Section -->
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="hero__container">
      <div class="hero__content">
        <p class="hero__label">2026 Manufacturing Comparison Guide</p>
        <h1 class="hero__title">Bangladesh vs Vietnam Clothing Manufacturing | The Complete Comparison for 2026</h1>
        <p class="hero__description">
          Bangladesh and Vietnam are the world's two largest garment exporters after China, each producing over $30 billion in apparel exports annually. Both countries offer compelling advantages for fashion brands, but the right choice depends on your specific needs. This comprehensive 2026 guide compares labor costs, minimum order quantities, trade agreements, lead times, sustainability certifications, and product specializations to help you make an informed sourcing decision. Understanding the real differences between these two manufacturing hubs matters for your supply chain strategy, whether this is your first collection or your tenth reorder. As a leading <a href="/clothing-manufacturer-bangladesh">clothing manufacturer Bangladesh</a>, SDF provides expert guidance on sourcing decisions.
        </p>
        <div class="hero__stats">
          <div class="hero__stat">
            <span class="hero__stat-value">$47B</span>
            <span class="hero__stat-label">Bangladesh Exports</span>
          </div>
          <div class="hero__stat">
            <span class="hero__stat-value">$44B</span>
            <span class="hero__stat-label">Vietnam Exports</span>
          </div>
          <div class="hero__stat">
            <span class="hero__stat-value">300</span>
            <span class="hero__stat-label">BD MOQ (pieces)</span>
          </div>
          <div class="hero__stat">
            <span class="hero__stat-value">500+</span>
            <span class="hero__stat-label">VN MOQ (pieces)</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="hero__ctas">
          <a href={whatsappQuoteUrl} class="hero__cta hero__cta--primary" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
            Get a Free Factory Quote
          </a>
          <a href="/contact/" class="hero__cta hero__cta--secondary">
            Contact Our Team
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="hero__image">
        <img src="/factory.jpg" alt="Clothing manufacturing facility comparison between Bangladesh and Vietnam" width="600" height="400" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Section 1: At a Glance Comparison Table -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="section__container">
      <h2 class="section__title">Bangladesh vs Vietnam | At a Glance (Key Metrics)</h2>
      <p class="section__text">
        This master comparison table provides the most important metrics side-by-side. Use this as your quick reference when evaluating sourcing options. Data sourced from World Bank, WTO, ILO, and industry reports for 2024-2025. For additional sourcing comparisons, see our <a href="/bangladesh-vs-india-clothing-manufacturing/">Bangladesh vs India manufacturing comparison</a>.
      </p>
      
      <div class="comparison-wrapper">
        <table class="comparison" role="table" aria-label="Bangladesh vs Vietnam manufacturing comparison table">
          <caption>Master comparison table of key manufacturing metrics between Bangladesh and Vietnam</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Factor</th>
              <th>Bangladesh</th>
              <th>Vietnam</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Garment Export Value (2024)</strong></td>
              <td>$47 billion</td>
              <td>$44 billion</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="comparison__row--highlight">
              <td><strong>MOQ Typical</strong></td>
              <td>300-500 pieces</td>
              <td>500-1,500 pieces</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Labor Cost (monthly)</strong></td>
              <td>$113 (garment sector)</td>
              <td>$150-192 (varies by region)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Lead Time to USA</strong></td>
              <td>40-45 days</td>
              <td>30-35 days</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Lead Time to EU</strong></td>
              <td>35-40 days</td>
              <td>25-30 days</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="comparison__row--highlight">
              <td><strong>US Tariff Rate</strong></td>
              <td>15-32% (MFN)</td>
              <td>15-32% (MFN)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>EU Tariff Rate</strong></td>
              <td>0% (EBA until Nov 2026)</td>
              <td>0% (EVFTA, rules of origin)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>GOTS Certified Factories</strong></td>
              <td>200+ facilities</td>
              <td>50+ facilities</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Minimum Wage (USD/month)</strong></td>
              <td>$113</td>
              <td>$150-192</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Main Product Specialization</strong></td>
              <td>Knitwear (70% of exports)</td>
              <td>Woven garments, footwear</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="comparison__row--highlight">
              <td><strong>FTA with EU</strong></td>
              <td>EBA (until Nov 2026)</td>
              <td>EVFTA (permanent)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>FTA with USA</strong></td>
              <td>None (MFN only)</td>
              <td>None (MFN only)</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      
      <p class="section__text">
        <em>Data sources: World Bank WTO Trade Data, ILO Wage Reports, BGMEA, VITAS. Export values are 2024 estimates. Tariff rates are approximate and vary by product category. Bangladesh EBA duty-free access expires November 2026 upon LDC graduation.</em>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Section 2: Labor Costs -->
  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="section__container">
      <h2 class="section__title">Labor Costs | Bangladesh vs Vietnam in 2026</h2>
      <p class="section__text">
        Labor cost is one of the most significant factors in manufacturing decisions. Both countries have seen wage increases in recent years, but Bangladesh maintains a clear advantage in absolute labor costs. However, Vietnam's higher worker efficiency partially offsets this disadvantage.
      </p>
      
      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Bangladesh Minimum Wage: $113/Month</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        Bangladesh's garment sector minimum wage was increased to $113 per month in 2023, up from $95 previously. This wage applies to entry-level garment workers in the export-oriented Ready-Made Garment (RMG) industry. While still low by global standards, this represents significant progress in worker compensation. The wage increase came after industry-wide negotiations between government, factory owners, and worker unions. For context, this wage is approximately one-third of China's garment sector wages and significantly below Vietnam's regional minimums.
      </p>
      
      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Vietnam Minimum Wage: $150-192/Month</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        Vietnam uses a regional minimum wage system with four zones. Region 1 (urban Hanoi and Ho Chi Minh City) has the highest minimum wage at approximately $192 per month. Region 4 (rural areas) has the lowest at approximately $150 per month. Most garment factories operate in Regions 2 and 3, where wages range from $165-180 monthly. Vietnam's higher wages reflect the country's more advanced economic development and higher cost of living. The government increases minimum wages annually, typically by 6%, keeping pace with inflation and economic growth.
      </p>
      
      <h3 class="section__subtitle">How Wage Differences Translate to Per-Unit Costs</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        The labor cost differential between Bangladesh and Vietnam typically translates to 10-15% lower FOB prices for basic garments from Bangladesh. For a basic t-shirt, Bangladesh might quote $3.50 while Vietnam quotes $4.00 for similar quality. However, this gap narrows for complex garments. Vietnam's higher worker efficiency (approximately 65% vs 40% in Bangladesh) means Vietnamese workers produce more pieces per hour, reducing the labor cost per unit. For technical outerwear or complex woven shirts requiring skilled labor, Vietnam's efficiency advantage can make their total cost competitive with or sometimes lower than Bangladesh.
      </p>
      
      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Labor Cost Trend: Both Rising, Bangladesh Still Lower</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        Both countries are experiencing upward wage pressure as their economies develop and workers demand better compensation. Bangladesh has seen larger percentage increases in recent years as the country catches up from a very low base. Vietnam's wage growth has been more steady and predictable. Industry analysts expect Bangladesh's labor cost advantage to persist through 2026, though the gap may narrow slightly. Brands should factor in potential wage increases when planning long-term sourcing strategies. For authoritative wage data, refer to <a href="https://www.ilo.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">International Labour Organization wage reports</a>, which provide comprehensive data on garment sector compensation across countries.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Section 3: MOQ Comparison -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="section__container">
      <h2 class="section__title">MOQ Comparison | Which Country Works for Startup Brands?</h2>
      <p class="section__text">
        Minimum Order Quantity (MOQ) is often the deciding factor for startup brands and small businesses. Bangladesh's lower MOQs provide significant flexibility for brands testing designs or launching capsule collections. Vietnam's higher MOQs reflect their focus on larger-scale production.
      </p>
      
      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Bangladesh Average MOQ: 300-500 Pieces</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        Bangladesh garment factories typically require 300-500 pieces per style, with flexible size and color breakdowns. SDF Clothing offers 300 piece minimums per style, making us accessible to emerging brands. This flexibility allows brands to test designs with manageable risk before committing to larger orders. Bangladesh's garment industry developed this low-MOQ capability to serve the mid-market segment that larger factories in China and Vietnam often overlook. Many Bangladesh factories specialize in working with startup brands and understand the needs of growing businesses.
      </p>
      
      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Vietnam Average MOQ: 500-1,500 Pieces</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        Vietnam factories generally require higher minimums, typically 500-1,500 pieces per style. This higher MOQ reflects Vietnam's production model focused on efficiency and scale. Vietnam's factories are often larger and more automated, making them less suitable for small orders. The higher MOQs also stem from Vietnam's focus on serving major global brands that place large, consistent orders. For established brands with proven demand and scale, Vietnam's MOQs are not a barrier. However, for startups and small businesses, these requirements can be prohibitive.
      </p>
      
      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Why Vietnam Factories Tend Toward Higher MOQ</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        Several factors explain Vietnam's higher MOQ requirements. First, Vietnam's garment industry is more vertically integrated with larger facilities optimized for high-volume production. Setting up production lines for small orders is less efficient in this model. Second, Vietnam has higher labor costs, so factories need larger orders to achieve economies of scale. Third, many Vietnamese factories work directly with major global brands and have structured their operations around large, recurring orders. Finally, Vietnam's supply chain for raw materials often has higher minimums, which cascades to garment manufacturers.
      </p>
      
      <h3 class="section__subtitle">For Startup Brands: Bangladesh Clear Advantage</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        For startup brands, boutique labels, and businesses testing new product lines, Bangladesh offers a clear MOQ advantage. The ability to order 300-500 pieces per style significantly reduces inventory risk and capital requirements. Brands can launch with smaller collections, test market response, and scale up gradually. This flexibility is particularly valuable for fashion brands operating in competitive, trend-driven markets where speed to market and testing multiple designs is critical. Bangladesh's low-MOQ manufacturers, including SDF Clothing, have built their businesses around serving this market segment. Learn more about <a href="/low-moq-clothing-manufacturers/">low MOQ clothing manufacturers</a> and how they can support your brand's growth.
      </p>
      
      <p class="section__text">
        For comprehensive guidance on working with manufacturers as a new brand, see our guide on <a href="/clothing-manufacturers-for-startups/">clothing manufacturers for startups</a>, which covers MOQ negotiation, payment terms, and building supplier relationships. If you need <a href="/private-label-clothing-manufacturer">private label manufacturing</a> services, Bangladesh offers excellent options for branded production. For full-package production including fabric sourcing, see our <a href="/full-package-production/">full package production</a> capabilities.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Section 4: Trade Agreements and Tariffs -->
  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="section__container">
      <h2 class="section__title">Trade Agreements and Tariff Comparison | USA, EU, UK, Australia</h2>
      <p class="section__text">
        Trade agreements and tariff rates significantly impact landed costs for imported clothing. Both Bangladesh and Vietnam have preferential access to certain markets, but the arrangements differ. Understanding these trade preferences is essential for accurate cost calculation.
      </p>
      
      <div class="comparison-wrapper">
        <table class="comparison" role="table" aria-label="Tariff comparison between Bangladesh and Vietnam by market">
          <caption>Tariff rates for Bangladesh and Vietnam across major markets</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Market</th>
              <th>Bangladesh Rate</th>
              <th>Vietnam Rate</th>
              <th>Difference</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>USA (MFN)</strong></td>
              <td>15-32%</td>
              <td>15-32%</td>
              <td>None</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="comparison__row--highlight">
              <td><strong>EU (GSP/EBA)</strong></td>
              <td>0% (until Nov 2026)</td>
              <td>0% (EVFTA, rules of origin)</td>
              <td>Temporary BD advantage</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>UK (DCTS)</strong></td>
              <td>0% (enhanced preferences)</td>
              <td>8-12%</td>
              <td>Bangladesh advantage</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Australia (DFAT)</strong></td>
              <td>0-5% (GSP)</td>
              <td>5-10%</td>
              <td>Bangladesh advantage</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      
      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Bangladesh LDC Status: 0% EU Duty Until November 2026</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        Bangladesh currently enjoys duty-free access to the European Union under the Everything But Arms (EBA) arrangement for Least Developed Countries. This provides 0% duty on essentially all apparel products, representing a significant cost advantage over competitors like China and Vietnam who face 9.6-12% EU tariffs. However, Bangladesh graduates from LDC status in November 2026, which will remove EBA benefits. The Bangladesh government is actively negotiating new trade agreements with the EU to maintain preferential access, but the terms are not yet finalized. Brands planning EU market entry should factor in this transition and consider both scenarios in their sourcing strategy.
      </p>
      
      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Vietnam EVFTA: 0% EU Duty for Qualifying Products</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        Vietnam's EU-Vietnam Free Trade Agreement (EVFTA) provides permanent duty-free access to the EU for qualifying products. Unlike Bangladesh's temporary EBA arrangement, EVFTA is a permanent free trade agreement. However, EVFTA includes rules of origin requirements - garments must use fabric produced in Vietnam or another EVFTA country to qualify for 0% duty. If fabric is sourced from China or other non-EVFTA countries, duties apply. Vietnam has been developing its textile industry to meet these rules of origin requirements, but some fabric categories still need to be imported. Brands sourcing from Vietnam for the EU market must verify their supply chain meets EVFTA origin rules to benefit from duty-free access.
      </p>
      
      <h3 class="section__subtitle">US: Neither Has Preferential Access | Both Pay MFN Rates</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        Neither Bangladesh nor Vietnam has a free trade agreement with the United States. Both countries pay Most Favored Nation (MFN) tariff rates, which range from 15-32% depending on product category. For basic garments like t-shirts and pants, the rate is typically 16.5%. For more complex categories like outerwear, rates can reach 32%. The absence of preferential US access means US-based brands should focus on other factors like MOQ, quality, and lead time when choosing between Bangladesh and Vietnam. Some US brands diversify sourcing across both countries to manage risk and take advantage of each country's strengths.
      </p>
      
      <h3 class="section__subtitle">UK DCTS: Bangladesh Gets Enhanced Preferences</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        The United Kingdom's Developing Countries Trading Scheme (DCTS) provides enhanced trade preferences for Bangladesh, including duty-free access for most apparel products. The UK scheme replaced the EU's GSP arrangement post-Brexit and maintains similar benefits for LDCs. Vietnam, as a middle-income country, receives standard DCTS preferences with duties of 8-12% on most apparel. For UK-focused brands, Bangladesh offers a clear tariff advantage that reduces landed costs. This advantage will persist beyond 2026 as DCTS is not tied to LDC graduation in the same way as EBA.
      </p>
      
      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Australia: Bangladesh Gets GSP Preferences</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        Australia's Generalized System of Preferences (GSP) scheme provides reduced or zero duties for imports from LDCs including Bangladesh. Most apparel from Bangladesh enters Australia duty-free or at minimal rates (0-5%). Vietnam, having graduated from LDC status, faces standard MFN duties of 5-10% on apparel imports. For Australian brands and retailers, Bangladesh's preferential access provides cost savings. The Australian market is relatively small but growing for ethical and sustainable fashion, where Bangladesh's certification advantages are also valuable.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Section 5: Product Specialization -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="section__container">
      <h2 class="section__title">Product Specialization | What Each Country Does Best</h2>
      <p class="section__text">
        While both countries can produce most garment types, each has developed specialization based on historical development, supply chain strengths, and market focus. Matching your product type to the appropriate country's strengths can improve quality and reduce costs.
      </p>
      
      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Bangladesh Strengths: Knitwear Dominance</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        Bangladesh is the world's leading producer of knitwear, which represents approximately 70% of the country's apparel exports. The country's garment industry grew around knitwear production, developing deep expertise in t-shirts, polo shirts, hoodies, sweatshirts, and activewear basics. Bangladesh's vertically integrated knitwear supply chain includes knitting, dyeing, and garment production under one roof, enabling cost efficiency and quality control. The country is also strong in denim production, with significant capacity for denim jeans, jackets, and casual wear. Basic woven garments like shirts and pants are also well-established categories.
      </p>
      
      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Vietnam Strengths: Woven Garments and Technical Products</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        Vietnam has developed particular strength in woven garments, including dress shirts, blouses, trousers, and jackets. The country's higher worker efficiency and attention to detail make it well-suited for garments requiring precision construction. Vietnam is also a major footwear producer, with significant capacity for shoes and sneakers that complements its apparel manufacturing. Technical outerwear, including performance jackets and sportswear, is another Vietnamese strength due to the country's experience with complex construction and technical fabrics. Vietnam also has a growing electronics manufacturing sector, which provides cross-industry technical expertise.
      </p>
      
      <div class="comparison-wrapper">
        <table class="comparison" role="table" aria-label="Product specialization comparison between Bangladesh and Vietnam">
