  <!-- Section 6: Lead Times -->
  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="section__container">
      <h2 class="section__title">Lead Times | Bangladesh vs Vietnam to Key Markets</h2>
      <p class="section__text">
        Lead time is a critical factor for fashion brands, especially those operating in fast-moving markets or with tight launch windows. Vietnam generally has shorter total lead times due to superior port infrastructure and customs efficiency, though production times are similar.
      </p>
      
      <div class="comparison-wrapper">
        <table class="comparison" role="table" aria-label="Lead time comparison from Bangladesh and Vietnam to key markets">
          <caption>Total lead time from order to delivery by destination</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Destination</th>
              <th>From Bangladesh</th>
              <th>From Vietnam</th>
              <th>Difference</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>USA West Coast</strong></td>
              <td>40-55 days</td>
              <td>30-45 days</td>
              <td>Vietnam 10-15 days faster</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>USA East Coast</strong></td>
              <td>45-60 days</td>
              <td>35-50 days</td>
              <td>Vietnam 10-15 days faster</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="comparison__row--highlight">
              <td><strong>UK</strong></td>
              <td>35-50 days</td>
              <td>25-40 days</td>
              <td>Vietnam 10-15 days faster</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Germany</strong></td>
              <td>35-50 days</td>
              <td>25-40 days</td>
              <td>Vietnam 10-15 days faster</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Australia</strong></td>
              <td>30-45 days</td>
              <td>20-35 days</td>
              <td>Vietnam 10-15 days faster</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Canada</strong></td>
              <td>45-60 days</td>
              <td>35-50 days</td>
              <td>Vietnam 10-15 days faster</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      
      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Production Lead Times Comparison</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        Production lead times are similar between the two countries, typically 30-45 days from fabric sourcing to finished goods ready for shipment. Bangladesh and Vietnam both have well-established production processes and can handle similar production volumes. Sample development takes 7-14 days in both countries. The difference in total lead time comes almost entirely from logistics and customs processes rather than production speed. Both countries can accommodate rush orders at higher cost, reducing production time to 20-25 days in some cases.
      </p>
      
      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Total Time from Order to Door Comparison</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        Vietnam's logistics advantage comes from several factors. First, Vietnam can release raw material imports at ports within 24 hours, while Bangladesh takes 48 hours to one month due to port congestion and customs processes. Second, Vietnam has direct deep-water ports that can accommodate mother vessels, while Bangladesh lacks a deep-sea port and requires transshipment through Singapore or Sri Lanka. This transshipment adds 5-7 days to Bangladesh's shipping time. Third, Vietnam's overall port infrastructure and customs efficiency are superior, reducing delays. For brands with tight deadlines or fast-fashion business models, Vietnam's 10-15 day lead time advantage can be significant. For brands with longer planning horizons, Bangladesh's cost advantages may outweigh the delivery time difference.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Section 7: Sustainability and Ethical Standards -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="section__container">
      <h2 class="section__title">Sustainability and Ethical Standards Comparison</h2>
      <p class="section__text">
        Sustainability and ethical manufacturing are increasingly important for fashion brands, especially those selling to EU and UK markets where consumers and retailers demand certified production. Both countries have made progress, but Bangladesh leads in certification counts and green factory development.
      </p>
      
      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Bangladesh: LEED-Certified Factories (Most in World)</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        Bangladesh has the highest number of LEED-certified green garment factories in the world, with over 200 facilities achieving platinum, gold, or silver certification. This leadership in green manufacturing came after the Rana Plaza tragedy in 2013, which spurred massive investment in factory safety and sustainability. The Bangladesh Garment Manufacturers and Exporters Association (BGMEA) actively promotes sustainable practices, and many factories have implemented water recycling, energy efficiency, and waste reduction systems. Bangladesh also has significant capacity for GOTS-certified organic cotton production, with over 200 GOTS-certified facilities. For brands requiring sustainability certifications, Bangladesh offers the largest pool of certified suppliers.
      </p>
      
      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Vietnam: Improving but Fewer Certified Facilities</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        Vietnam is improving its sustainability performance but has fewer certified facilities compared to Bangladesh. The country has approximately 50 GOTS-certified facilities and a growing number of factories pursuing LEED certification. Vietnam's garment industry association (VITAS) has launched sustainability initiatives, and major factories are investing in environmental improvements. However, Vietnam's focus on efficiency and cost competitiveness has historically prioritized other factors over sustainability certification. This is changing as EU and US buyers increasingly demand certified production, but Bangladesh currently holds an advantage in certified supplier availability.
      </p>
      
      <h3 class="section__subtitle">EU Brands Increasingly Demanding Sustainability | Bangladesh Advantage</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        European brands and retailers are under increasing pressure from consumers and regulators to source from sustainable suppliers. The EU's forthcoming textile strategy and due diligence regulations will require brands to verify environmental and social standards throughout their supply chain. Bangladesh's large pool of certified factories makes it easier for brands to meet these requirements. Many Bangladesh factories already have the certifications EU buyers demand, including GOTS, OEKO-TEX, BSCI, WRAP, and LEED. For brands targeting the EU market, Bangladesh's certification advantage reduces compliance risk and simplifies supplier selection. Learn more about <a href="/sustainable-clothing-manufacturers/">sustainable clothing manufacturing</a> options in Bangladesh.
      </p>
      
      <p class="section__text">
        For detailed information about the certifications available from Bangladesh manufacturers, see our <a href="/certifications">certifications page</a>, which covers GOTS, OEKO-TEX, BSCI, WRAP, ISO standards, and other relevant certifications. If you're also considering <a href="/clothing-manufacturer-china">clothing manufacturer China</a> as an option, our comparison guide can help. For a deeper dive into Bangladesh vs China specifically, see <a href="/bangladesh-vs-china-clothing-manufacturing">Bangladesh vs China clothing manufacturing</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Section 8: When to Choose Each Country -->
  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="section__container">
      <h2 class="section__title">When to Choose Bangladesh vs Vietnam</h2>
      <p class="section__text">
        Based on the comparison above, here's a decision framework to help you choose the right manufacturing country for your specific situation. The right choice depends on your brand's priorities, target market, product type, and scale.
      </p>
      
      <div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: var(--spacing-md); margin-top: var(--spacing-md);">
        <div style="background: var(--color-bg-alt); padding: var(--spacing-md); border: 1px solid var(--color-border); border-radius: 8px;">
          <h3 class="section__subtitle" style="margin-top: 0;">Choose Bangladesh When:</h3>
          <ul style="color: var(--color-text-muted); line-height: 1.8; padding-left: var(--spacing-md);">
            <li>You are a startup brand needing low MOQ (300-500 pieces)</li>
            <li>You are an EU buyer benefiting from duty-free EBA access (until Nov 2026)</li>
            <li>Your product focus is knitwear, t-shirts, or denim</li>
            <li>You need sustainability certifications (GOTS, OEKO-TEX, LEED)</li>
            <li>Cost is your primary driver and you can accept longer lead times</li>
            <li>You need flexible size and color breakdowns within smaller orders</li>
            <li>You are targeting the UK or Australian markets with preferential access</li>
            <li>You value Bangladesh's 25+ years of garment export experience</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        
        <div style="background: var(--color-bg-alt); padding: var(--spacing-md); border: 1px solid var(--color-border); border-radius: 8px;">
          <h3 class="section__subtitle" style="margin-top: 0;">Choose Vietnam When:</h3>
          <ul style="color: var(--color-text-muted); line-height: 1.8; padding-left: var(--spacing-md);">
            <li>You require high-volume woven production (1,000+ pieces per style)</li>
            <li>Your products are technical outerwear or complex woven garments</li>
            <li>You have existing Vietnam supply chain relationships</li>
            <li>You are a US brand wanting to diversify away from China</li>
            <li>Speed is critical and you need 10-15 day lead time advantage</li>
            <li>Your products meet EVFTA rules of origin for EU duty-free access</li>
            <li>You need higher precision construction for dress shirts or formal wear</li>
            <li>You are producing footwear in addition to apparel</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      
      <p class="section__text" style="margin-top: var(--spacing-md);">
        Many successful brands diversify sourcing across both countries to leverage each country's strengths. For example, a brand might source knitwear from Bangladesh and woven shirts from Vietnam. This diversification also reduces supply chain risk by not depending on a single country.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Section 9: FAQ -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="section__container">
      <h2 class="section__title">Bangladesh vs Vietnam FAQ</h2>
      
      <div class="faq">
        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">Is Bangladesh or Vietnam cheaper for clothing manufacturing?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">
            Bangladesh is generally cheaper for clothing manufacturing due to lower labor costs. Bangladesh's minimum wage for garment workers is approximately $113 per month, while Vietnam ranges from $150-192 monthly depending on region. This labor advantage translates to 10-15% lower FOB prices for basic garments from Bangladesh. However, Vietnam can be competitive for complex woven garments where higher efficiency offsets labor costs.
          </p>
        </div>
        
        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">Which country has lower MOQ | Bangladesh or Vietnam?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">
            Bangladesh has lower minimum order quantities. Typical Bangladesh MOQs range from 300-500 pieces per style, with SDF Clothing offering 300 piece minimums. Vietnam factories typically require 500-1,500 pieces per style. Vietnam's higher MOQs reflect their focus on larger orders and higher production efficiency. For startup brands and small businesses, Bangladesh's lower MOQs provide significant flexibility.
          </p>
        </div>
        
        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">Does Bangladesh have duty-free access to the EU?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">
            Yes, Bangladesh currently enjoys duty-free access to the EU under the Everything But Arms (EBA) arrangement for least developed countries. This provides 0% duty on most apparel products. Bangladesh graduates from LDC status in November 2026, which will change this arrangement. Vietnam has the EU-Vietnam Free Trade Agreement (EVFTA) offering 0% duty for qualifying products meeting rules of origin requirements.
          </p>
        </div>
        
        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">What clothing is Bangladesh best at manufacturing?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">
            Bangladesh specializes in knitwear, which represents approximately 70% of the country's apparel exports. Key strengths include t-shirts, hoodies, polo shirts, activewear basics, denim, fleece, and basic woven garments. Bangladesh has developed deep expertise in knitwear production with vertically integrated operations covering knitting, dyeing, and garment production. The country is also strong in basic woven shirts and pants.
          </p>
        </div>
        
        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">How do lead times compare between Bangladesh and Vietnam?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">
            Vietnam generally has shorter lead times due to better port infrastructure and customs efficiency. Vietnam can release raw material imports within 24 hours and deliver to European buyers 10-15 days faster than Bangladesh. Bangladesh takes 48 hours to one month to release raw material imports due to port congestion. Production lead times are similar (30-45 days), but Vietnam's logistics advantage gives them an overall edge in total delivery time.
          </p>
        </div>
        
        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">Is quality better in Bangladesh or Vietnam?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">
            Both countries produce high-quality garments, but they excel in different areas. Vietnam has higher worker efficiency (65% vs 40% in Bangladesh) and often performs better on quality consistency metrics for complex woven garments. Bangladesh has made significant quality improvements post-Rana Plaza and excels at knitwear quality. Bangladesh leads in sustainability certifications with the most LEED-certified green factories globally. Quality ultimately depends on the specific factory, not the country.
          </p>
        </div>
        
        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">Can small brands work with Bangladesh factories?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">
            Yes, small brands can work with Bangladesh factories, and Bangladesh is often more accommodating than Vietnam for small orders. Many Bangladesh manufacturers, including SDF Clothing, specialize in low MOQ production with 300 piece minimums. Bangladesh factories offer greater flexibility in order quantity and size breakdowns. Vietnam factories typically focus on larger orders due to their higher efficiency model and higher MOQ requirements.
          </p>
        </div>
        
        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">What happens to Bangladesh trade preferences after 2026?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">
            Bangladesh graduates from Least Developed Country status in November 2026, which will remove EU duty-free access under EBA. However, Bangladesh is negotiating new trade agreements with the EU and other markets. The government is preparing for this transition by improving productivity, negotiating bilateral agreements, and encouraging factories to obtain certifications that will be valuable in a post-LDC environment. Many manufacturers are already adapting to the changing trade landscape.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

